{"parentUuid":"8a976468-f68f-42d5-9a4c-dce39353334f","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"c1ed167a-e051-4050-bb99-05ea85646cca","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a4d6301","slug":"gentle-weaving-manatee","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_017P5ZDJU61Vgw61fALFk68P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01UQUQryk8HFaxoD7LcfR9FY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app/c1ed167a-e051-4050-bb99-05ea85646cca/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01YCUEsCrfmJPpXccX1RAv9w.txt","offset":400,"limit":400}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6019,"cache_read_input_tokens":34553,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6019,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXtDCoqKsxz6XWZStUKgw","type":"assistant","uuid":"8d9545f6-ad50-4033-bd02-38c6e2561b03","timestamp":"2026-02-07T06:10:09.462Z"}
{"parentUuid":"8d9545f6-ad50-4033-bd02-38c6e2561b03","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"c1ed167a-e051-4050-bb99-05ea85646cca","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a4d6301","slug":"gentle-weaving-manatee","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01UQUQryk8HFaxoD7LcfR9FY","type":"tool_result","content":"   400→easiest way to understand each of these heating mode \n   401→is through example. \n   402→CONVECTION \n   403→Convection heat transfer occurs when a fluid (such a \n   404→air, nitrogen, or water) passes over a solid (such as a \n   405→SMT assembly). A cool breeze on a hot sunny da \n   406→provides convective cooling. Hot air from a hair drye \n   407→provides convective heating. Convection heating c \n   408→cooling requires contact of the flow with the solid par \n   409→Only the layer of the flow that is in contact with the pa \n   410→is actually transferring heat. \n   411→2ONVECTION I - \n   412→HOT . \n   413→AIR \\ \n   414→FLOW - COLD OBJECT IS - HEATED BY CONVECTION \n   415→FROM THE HOT AIR FLOW \n   416→CONDUCTION \n   417→Conduction heat transfer occurs when two solid masse \n   418→of different temperature are in contact with each other \n   419→A good example is when a pan is placed on an electril \n   420→burner. Most of the heat is transferred to the pan by thl \n   421→contact between the pan and the burner. Conduction alsc \n   422→occurs within the same mass if a temperature differen \n   423→tial exists within the mass. \n   424→Referring to a system as 100% convection or \n   425→100% IR is incorrect. A system may be dominated \n   426→by convection or IR, but the other heat transfer is \n   427→always present in signijicant quantities. CONDUCTION \n   428→HEAT FLOWS FROM HOT TO COLD TO HELP \n   429→EQUALIZE TEMPERATURES \n   430→RADIATION \n   431→Infrared Radiation (IR) occurs when two bodies of dif- \n   432→ferent temperature are in sight of each other. The best \n   433→example of IR is the heating of the earth by the sun. IR \n   434→is a non-contact heat transfer. \n   435→RADIATION I \n   436→In SMT reflow applications (with the exception of vapor \n   437→phase), convection, conduction, and IR all play a role in \n   438→the heating process. Referring to a system as 100% \n   439→convection or 100% IR is incorrect. A system may be \n   440→dominated by convection or IR, but the other heat trans- \n   441→fer is always present. IR dominant systems usually range \n   442→in the convection/IR ratio of 60/40 to 40/60. Convection \n   443→dominant systems have ratios in the range of 70/30 to \n   444→80/20. Conduction heat transfer occurs more within the \n   445→product during the heating process. Heat is transferred \n   446→through the product to help minimize hot or cold spots. \n   447→Section I - Technology 7 \n   448→\n   449→================================================================================\n   450→PAGE 10\n   451→================================================================================\n   452→HOW DOES INFRARED HEATING WORK? \n   453→95.38 Infrared (IR) heat transfer occurs when two objects at \n   454→different temperatures are in sight of each other. The \n   455→heat is transferred by electromagnetic waves of 0.78- \n   456→1000 micron wavelengths. All objects emit some level \n   457→of infrared. The quantity of infrared energy emitted and \n   458→the wavelength of the emission are both dependent upon \n   459→the absolute temperature of the object. As the source \n   460→temperature increases, the heat transfer output increases \n   461→exponentially to the fourth power. Increasing the source \n   462→temperature results in shorter wavelengths. Decreasing \n   463→the source temperature results in longer wavelengths. In \n   464→SMT reflow the amount of heat transfer is important, so \n   465→as not to exceed the 2OC/sec rate. When operating at \n   466→these low heating rates all types of radiant emitters \n   467→(lamps, panels, tubes) operate in the medium to long \n   468→wavelength ranges. \n   469→111111lI INFRARED HEATING EQUATION \n   470→In order to understand what parameters are important in \n   471→infrared heating, one can consider the general equation \n   472→for heat transfer between the heat source and the object \n   473→being heated. Again, the purpose is not to memorize the \n   474→equation, but rather to point out the significance of what \n   475→is involved. The general equation for heat transfer is as \n   476→follows: \n   477→Q = V x Es x At x K x (Ts4 - Tt4) \n   478→WHERE: \n   479→Q = Infrared heat transfer (w/cm2) \n   480→V = Geometric view factor (0-1) \n   481→Es = Emissivity factor of the source (0-1) \n   482→At = Absorptivity factor of the target (0-1) \n   483→K = Stefan-Boltzmann Constant \n   484→Ts = Source temperature (OK) \n   485→Tt = Target temperature (OK) (5.67 x w/cm2/”K4) \n   486→Absorption characteristics of solder \n   487→paste and circuit board material makes \n   488→IR an efficient means of heating. The geometric view factor “V” is the fraction of energy \n   489→that leaves the source that hits the target. In SMTreflow, \n   490→the oven chamber designs yield very high view factors \n   491→in the range of 0.90 to 0.95. An important aspect of the \n   492→view factor comes in product design. If two very large \n   493→components are in close proximity to each other, the \n   494→view factor to a solder joint between them is decreased, \n   495→which makes it more difficult. Refer to figure. \n   496→f IRSOURCE \n   497→THIS AREA IS SHADED BY / \n   498→ADJACENT COMPONENTS \n   499→RESULTING IN A LOWER VIEW \n   500→FACTOR \n   501→The source emissivity and the target absorptivity factors \n   502→are in the range on 0.90 to 0.95 for most SMT applica- \n   503→tions. Solder paste is an excellent absorber of infrared \n   504→energy. Shiny gold components may be difficult to heat \n   505→as they tend to be reflective. Most often, however, the \n   506→board material, solder paste, and the components all \n   507→absorb quite well. The figure below shows the spectral \n   508→absorptivity for an RMA solder paste.(3) \n   509→I \n   510→loooo t \n   511→! 97.39 I4 I \n   512→96’70 96.04 I! \n   513→Control of infrared is generally done by controlling the \n   514→source temperature. Providing that the overall source \n   515→emission can be regulated, IR can provide high levels of \n   516→repeatability. \n   517→8 Section I - Technology \n   518→\n   519→================================================================================\n   520→PAGE 11\n   521→================================================================================\n   522→HOW DOES CONVECTION HEATING WORK? \n   523→Convection heat transfer occurs when a fluid at a given \n   524→temperature contacts a solid mass at a different tempera- \n   525→ture. If the fluid is hotter than the mass, the mass will be \n   526→heated. If the fluid is cooler than the mass, the mass will \n   527→be cooled. Perhaps the easiest way to understand convec- \n   528→tion is to look at the convection equation and note what \n   529→is important and what is not important. \n   530→CONVECTION EQUATION \n   531→The following is the general equation for convection \n   532→heating or cooling: \n   533→Q=HxAT \n   534→WHERE: \n   535→Q = Heat absorbed per unit area (w/cm2) \n   536→H = Convective film coefficient \n   537→AT = Temperature difference between (W/cm2-”C) \n   538→fluid and object (“C) \n   539→The amount of heat transfer “Q’ can be modified by \n   540→either changing the convective coefficient “H’ or the \n   541→temperature difference “AT”. Increasing the source \n   542→temperature will increase the temperature difference, \n   543→and thus the amount of heat transfer “Q’ will increase \n   544→proportionally. \n   545→The convective coefficient “H’ can have a significant \n   546→impact on the heat transfer “Q”. The value of “H” is \n   547→related to a number of variables, including velocity of \n   548→the flow and angle of attack of the flow. \n   549→Increasing the flow velocity will increase the value of \n   550→“H”. For example, when one is outside in the wind, a \n   551→low velocity cool breeze on a warm day will feel quite \n   552→nice. However, a strong wind on that same day may feel \n   553→~ ~~ ~~ \n   554→Perpendicular flow convection breaks \n   555→up surface thermal boundary layers, \n   556→which enhances heat transfer. very cold. That is because the higher velocity wind has \n   557→a higher convective coefficient, and thus removes heat \n   558→more effectively from your skin. In SMT reflow appli- \n   559→cations, increasing the velocity of the flow also improves \n   560→the amount of convection heat transfer. There are prac- \n   561→tical limits to this, as too high of velocity may cause \n   562→components to shift around. The flow direction also has \n   563→a significant impact on “Q’. Parallel flow convection \n   564→is shown below: \n   565→PARALLEL FLOW WIBOUNDARY LAYER \n   566→I FLOW DIRECTION 4 - \n   567→BOUNDARY LAYER FORMATION \n   568→CAUSES STAGNANT FLOW NEAR \n   569→BOARD, WHICH REDUCES \n   570→HEAT TRANSFER \n   571→Note that in the parallel flow diagram, there is an area \n   572→referred to as the “boundary layer”. In the boundary \n   573→layer, there is no flow motion, which causes convective \n   574→heat transfer to be very low. It is beneficial to break up \n   575→the boundary layer to allow the flow to transfer heat \n   576→freely to the object. The diagram shown below shows \n   577→perpendicular flow convection: \n   578→PERPENDICULAR FLOW \n   579→FLOW DIRECTION \n   580→ttt \n   581→NO BOUNDARY \n   582→LAYER \n   583→NO BOUNDARY LAYER FORMATION \n   584→ENHANCES CONVECTIVE HEAT \n   585→TRANSFER \n   586→In the diagram showing perpendicular flow, note that \n   587→there is no boundary layer. This is the reason that \n   588→perpendicular flow yields better convective heating or \n   589→cooling results. \n   590→Section I - Technology 9 \n   591→\n   592→================================================================================\n   593→PAGE 12\n   594→================================================================================\n   595→WHAT ARE THE BENEFITS OF IR? ... CONVECTION? \n   596→In SMT reflow applications, there is no such thing as \n   597→pure convection or pure IR. The only way to achieve a \n   598→100% convection system would be to have gas flowing \n   599→over the product with no chamber walls. Of course this \n   600→in not practical. To have a 100% IR system, the chamber \n   601→would need to be operated in a vacuum. Again, this is not \n   602→the case. However, some systems are more dominated by \n   603→convection, while others utilize IR as the primary heat \n   604→source. For the sake of simplicity this text will refer to a \n   605→convection dominated machine as a convection ma- \n   606→chine, and an IR dominate machine as an IR machine. \n   607→CONVECTION \n   608→Both convection and IR have their unique advantages \n   609→and limitations. In convective systems, typically the \n   610→temperature of the flow is close to the desired tempera- \n   611→ture of the product in a given zone. This has the \n   612→advantage in that the product temperature will never \n   613→exceed the gas flow temperature. This is useful when \n   614→heating a product that has a large difference in the mass \n   615→of the components and distribution. In this case, the \n   616→larger components will continue to heat, while the smaller \n   617→components will not overheat. Convection heat transfer \n   618→is not thermally efficient. Air (or nitrogen) has a very low \n   619→heat capacity, which means that high volumes of flow \n   620→may be required to transfer the heat. One way to improve \n   621→efficiency and reduce the amount of process gas required \n   622→CONVECTION \n   623→Less sensitive to shading \n   624→Heats more uniformly \n   625→AT (Source-target) is small \n   626→Low thermal mass of air requires \n   627→high flow rates to transfer heat \n   628→Inefficient (20 to 30%) \n   629→Will heat leads first, increasing \n   630→chance of solder wicking. is through the use of recirculation. In a recirculation \n   631→loop, the process gas from a zone is evacuated, then \n   632→reheated and re-input into the heating chamber. The \n   633→limitation to recirculating is that high nitrogen purity \n   634→levels are not typically attainable. There is also a concern \n   635→that if the gas is recirculated excessively, it may affect \n   636→soldering quality, as the atmosphere surrounding the \n   637→product becomes contaminated with process by-prod- \n   638→ucts. Convection increases the chance of solder wicking, \n   639→as typically the lead is heated faster than the circuit board. \n   640→IR \n   641→IR systems have the distinct advantage of high heating \n   642→efficiencies and low process gas flows required to reflow \n   643→solder. IR systems are normally very repeatable, and \n   644→have the capability to transfer high quantities of heat as \n   645→needed. This becomes quite useful when reflowing very \n   646→dense SMT assemblies or very heavy boards. The \n   647→limitation with IR is that a high temperature differential \n   648→between the source and the product is required to transfer \n   649→the heat. Products with high component mass differen- \n   650→tial do not heat uniformly. They must be run quite slowly \n   651→to accomplish reflow. IR systems are successful at \n   652→reflowing products with component sizes up to and \n   653→including 84 pin PLCC’s. Large connectors and pin grid \n   654→arrays are quite difficult to reflow on an IR dominated \n   655→system. \n   656→IR \n   657→Control source temperature only \n   658→High efficiency (60-70%) \n   659→Easy to control rate of rise \n   660→More sensitive to shading \n   661→Less uniform high mass differences \n   662→AT (Source-target) is large \n   663→10 Section I - Technology \n   664→\n   665→================================================================================\n   666→PAGE 13\n   667→================================================================================\n   668→WHAT ARE THE BENEFITS OF A NITROGEN ATMOSPHERE? \n   669→~ \n   670→Ease of cleaning flux \n   671→residues (RMA) Reflow soldering in a nitrogen atmosphere is becoming \n   672→a significant process consideration. The issue is really \n   673→not the ability to reflow in nitrogen, but rather the ability \n   674→to reflow in the absence of oxygen. Heating solder in \n   675→the presence of oxygen will create oxides, which are \n   676→generally non-solderable surfaces. In past days of reflow \n   677→soldering, the solder paste consisted of aggressive enough \n   678→fluxes to remove oxides during the reflow process. The \n   679→future trend is moving away from strong fluxes in the \n   680→paste. The future trends include two major process \n   681→changes that are driving the need for reflow in nitrogen \n   682→atmospheres; fine pitch soldering and no-clean fluxes. \n   683→500 ppm FINE PITCH REFLOW SOLDERING \n   684→Fine pitch soldering presents many challenges. In order \n   685→to obtain satisfactory paste print definition, the particle \n   686→sizes of tin and lead are becoming smaller. As the \n   687→particles become smaller, the ratio of surface area to \n   688→volume of the particles increases. This can be shown by: \n   689→RATIO = (n x D*) /(1/6 x n x D3) \n   690→RATIO = 6 / D \n   691→WHERE: \n   692→D = Particle Diameter \n   693→FOR THE SAME VOLUME (OR MASS) OF SOLDER, THERE IS \n   694→GREATER SURFACE AREA WITH SMALLER PARTICLES THAN \n   695→WITH LARGER PARTICLES. \n   696→Thus, as particles sizes decrease, the ratio increases. \n   697→With more available surface area in the solder paste, \n   698→there is a greater tendency towards oxidation. Keep in \n   699→mind that oxidation is a function of the metals involved, \n   700→the temperatures of the metal, the exposed surface area, \n   701→and the amount of oxygen present. The only variable \n   702→that one can control is the amount of oxygen present. \n   703→This can only be done through proper oven design. NO-CLEAN SOLDERING \n   704→The second technology change that is driving the need \n   705→towards nitrogen atmospheres is the greater usage of no- \n   706→clean pastes. The rapid trend away from solvent cleaning \n   707→is creating this and other needs. No-clean soldering \n   708→requires a tight control of the process. All of the compo- \n   709→nents and boards must be highly solderable. The reflow \n   710→process must not add any significant oxidation to the \n   711→process. The fluxes used are very weak, and do not have \n   712→aggressive oxide cleaning action. In addition, there is no \n   713→cleaning that would remove any solder balls from the \n   714→product. These solder balls eventually can create short \n   715→circuits on the product if they are not removed. Reflowing \n   716→in a high purity nitrogen environment reduces the possi- \n   717→bility of creating solder balls. \n   718→A good question to ask is “How pure a nitrogen environ- \n   719→ment is required?” A recent study (4) by Colin Lea of the \n   720→National Physics Laboratory in the United Kingdom was \n   721→conducted to determine the affect of nitrogen purity on \n   722→the process. The conclusions drawn in the study are \n   723→listed below: \n   724→DESCRIPTION I PURITY LEVEL I \n   725→Significant reduction \n   726→in board discoloration 10,000 ppm \n   727→Reduction in solder 200 ppm \n   728→balling (RMA) \n   729→Reflow of low-residue \n   730→solder paste < 100 ppm \n   731→The paper presents a number of excellent graphs that \n   732→show the affect of nitrogen purity on parameters includ- \n   733→ing flux spread, wetting force, wetting time, solder balls, \n   734→cleaning, residue, board discoloration, and micro-struc- \n   735→ture. It is recommended reading if your process may \n   736→require the use of nitrogen. You must determine the \n   737→nitrogen requirements for your process. The benefits of \n   738→using nitrogen definitely exist, however you must deter- \n   739→mine if the additional cost of nitrogen is worthwhile for \n   740→your process. \n   741→Section I - Technology 11 \n   742→\n   743→================================================================================\n   744→PAGE 14\n   745→================================================================================\n   746→1 SECTION II - EQUIPMENT SELECTION I \n   747→WHAT ARE THE STEPS IN THE SELECTION PROCESS? \n   748→When selecting reflow equipment, it is important to take \n   749→into consideration all aspects that may affect the process \n   750→and production. Try to avoid becoming too focussed on \n   751→one issue, such as populated uniformity, as it may not \n   752→lead you to the optimum decision. The recommended \n   753→steps in the selection process are described in the follow- \n   754→ing paragraphs. \n   755→DEFINE PROCESS BAND WIDTH \n   756→The first step is to define the acceptable process profile \n   757→band width for your SMT assemblies. Try to be as \n   758→accurate as possible when defining the band. If the band \n   759→is too restrictive, some aspect of production will be \n   760→negatively affected. If the band is too loose, a defect will \n   761→show up in the process. The band width should be \n   762→defined as a range of temperatures (\"C) that the entire \n   763→profile must fall within. Refer to page 4 for a more \n   764→detailed description. \n   765→DEFINE HEATING NON-UNIFORMITIES \n   766→The next step is to define causes of heating non-unifor- \n   767→mities that may occur in the process. These non-unifor- \n   768→mities are caused by mass differential, oven uniformity, \n   769→and oven repeatability under production loading condi- \n   770→tions. A set of tests must be conducted with the desired \n   771→product on the oven in question. The sum of the non- \n   772→uniformities (\"C) must be less than the profile band width \n   773→defined in the first step. Refer to page 5 for a more \n   774→detailed description. \n   775→SIZE THE OVEN PROPERLY \n   776→The oven must be sized to handle the production rate of \n   777→the pick-and-place equipment. The calculation is shown \n   778→below: \n   779→LINE SPEED - BOARDS PER MINUTE X LENGTH PER BOARD \n   780→(MINIMUM) - LOAD FACTOR \n   781→Operating at a conveyor speed greater than the calculated \n   782→speed ensures that no product jams will occur on the \n   783→onload area of the reflow oven. \n   784→Try to avoid becoming too focussed on one \n   785→issue, such as populated uniformity, as it may \n   786→not lead you to the optimum decision. In order for the oven to be properly sized, the process \n   787→speed defined below must be greater than the minimum \n   788→calculated line speed. This will ensure that bottlenecks \n   789→will not occur at the reflow oven. The process speed \n   790→should be determined through testing or by the following \n   791→equation: \n   792→OVEN CHAMBER HEATED LENGTH PROCESS - \n   793→SPEED - PROCESS DWELL TIME \n   794→DEFINE PROCESS GAS \n   795→If a nitrogen atmosphere is required for solderability or \n   796→no-clean processing (see page lo), one needs to consider \n   797→the purity required and the gas consumption. Be careful \n   798→how the purity is measured, such that it is not measured \n   799→at the gas inlet. \n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"37a605c4-449d-4e2e-9d68-55526281129c","timestamp":"2026-02-07T06:10:09.852Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8d9545f6-ad50-4033-bd02-38c6e2561b03"}
