{"parentUuid":"29726878-2d03-436f-8678-85e7f8d25613","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"c1ed167a-e051-4050-bb99-05ea85646cca","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a4d6301","slug":"gentle-weaving-manatee","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01ULXJ1SedioeCXC4dFvUkLX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01QF85DcXCfzUZfvQpunPQc2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app/c1ed167a-e051-4050-bb99-05ea85646cca/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01YCUEsCrfmJPpXccX1RAv9w.txt","offset":800,"limit":400}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6172,"cache_read_input_tokens":40572,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6172,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXtDDFmJ8yFuWfgt1LoLm","type":"assistant","uuid":"152169ae-16da-4fe3-8133-0afad6f5587e","timestamp":"2026-02-07T06:10:15.882Z"}
{"parentUuid":"152169ae-16da-4fe3-8133-0afad6f5587e","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"c1ed167a-e051-4050-bb99-05ea85646cca","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a4d6301","slug":"gentle-weaving-manatee","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01QF85DcXCfzUZfvQpunPQc2","type":"tool_result","content":"   800→REVIEW CONVEYOR DESIGN \n   801→The conveying mechanism is perhaps the most over- \n   802→looked aspect of equipment selection. One needs to \n   803→define whether a mesh conveyor or an edge type is \n   804→required. Typically edge conveyors are used for in-line \n   805→processing or if double-sided assembly is required. The \n   806→most important issue with edge conveyors is the ability \n   807→for the conveyor to remain straight to avoid dropping \n   808→product. \n   809→RELIABILITY/SERVICEABILITY \n   810→Once a machine is placed into service, it is critical that it \n   811→remains operational. Evaluate the product design from a \n   812→structural as well as a functional standpoint. Is the oven \n   813→designed to withstand the rigors of production? Review \n   814→the product manuals, drawings, and spare parts lists. \n   815→Find out where the nearest servicing center is located. Is \n   816→training available? These are the questions that must be \n   817→asked during the selection process. \n   818→INTERFACING REQUIREMENTS \n   819→Conveyor and control interfacing requirements may also \n   820→be an important selection issue. Make sure that the edge \n   821→conveyor fixed rail justification is the same as the pick- \n   822→and-place equipment. Ask questions about networking \n   823→machines to a host and product flow control schemes if \n   824→needed. \n   825→12 Section I1 - Equipment Selection \n   826→\n   827→================================================================================\n   828→PAGE 15\n   829→================================================================================\n   830→HOW CAN YOU EVALUATE COSTS? \n   831→DESCRIPTION The selection steps listed on the previous page should \n   832→all be considered when comparing equipment. When \n   833→multiple ovens pass the selection criteria listed, then a \n   834→proper cost evaluation may be used to compare ovens. \n   835→This is especially important when comparing ovens that \n   836→utilize different heating technologies, as first cost and \n   837→operating costs can be significantly different. This \n   838→section describes various costs that may be included in \n   839→the analysis. \n   840→HOT GAS IR EQUIPMENT COST \n   841→Often, the initial cost of the equipment is the only cost \n   842→considered when comparing equipment. It is certainly \n   843→important, although it is only a part of the total cost \n   844→picture. To make a fair comparison, all costs should be \n   845→analyzed with a common basis, such as annual cost. As \n   846→an example let’s look at amachine that costs $50,000 and \n   847→has an expected life of 5 years, with an interest rate of \n   848→10%. Assume the value of the equipment is zero at the \n   849→end of five years. The annualized cost of the equipment \n   850→is equal to the initial cost times the Capital Recover \n   851→Factor. (3 For an interest rate of 10% with a period of \n   852→5 years, the CRF value is equal to 0.2638. The annu- \n   853→alized cost is then equal to: \n   854→Annual Cost = $50,000 x 0.2638 \n   855→= $13,190 ~ ~ \n   856→GAS COST \n   857→PER 1000 CFH \n   858→OPERATING COST \n   859→The operating cost needs to include the electrical power \n   860→to operate the machine and also the cost of any process \n   861→gases (such as nitrogen). If there are other pertinent \n   862→operating costs, include them in this analysis. $5.00 \n   863→When multiple ovens pass the selection criteria \n   864→listed, then aproper cost evaluation may be used \n   865→to compare ovens. POWER COST \n   866→PER KW-HR \n   867→The importance of factoring in operating costs \n   868→should be noted. $0.10 MAINTENANCE AND SPARE PARTS COSTS \n   869→The maintenance and spare parts costs should be in- \n   870→cluded in the analysis. This may be the most difficult to \n   871→predict, but it should be included. \n   872→SUMMARY - \n   873→An example of an annual cost analysis is shown in the \n   874→table below, comparing a convection (hot gas) machine \n   875→to an IR machine. The values used are not specific to any \n   876→vendor’s equipment or products, merely typical values \n   877→from the market. The total annual estimated costs reveal \n   878→the importance of including the operational costs in the \n   879→analysis. - \n   880→- \n   881→CRF \n   882→(FROM ABOVE) 0.2638 0.2638 GAS USAGE I 1800CFH I 1200CFH I \n   883→EQUIPMENT $13,190 \n   884→GAS COST $18,720 \n   885→POWER COST $4,160 POWERUSAGE I 20KW I 12KW I \n   886→$15,828 \n   887→$12,480 \n   888→$2,496 HOURSEEAR 2080 HRS 2080 HRS -+-I--- INITIAL COST $50,000 $60,000 \n   889→MAINTENANCE \n   890→TOTAL $ 1,000 $1,000 \n   891→$37,070 $31,804 $5.00 \n   892→$0.10 \n   893→Section I1 - Equipment Selection 13 \n   894→\n   895→================================================================================\n   896→PAGE 16\n   897→================================================================================\n   898→SELECTION CRITERIA CHART \n   899→PROCESS VARIABLE VENDOR A VENDOR B VENDOR C \n   900→PROCESS BAND WIDTH REQUIRED (\"C) \n   901→H EAT1 NG NON-U N I FO RMlTl ES \n   902→MASS DIFFERENTIAL (\"C) \n   903→OVEN UNIFORMITY (\"C) \n   904→I REPEATABILITY (\"C) I I 1 \n   905→MAXIMUM LOADING FACTOR \n   906→PROCESS SPEED I \n   907→PROCESS GAS TYPE \n   908→DRYOUT ZONE PURITY (PPM) \n   909→I PROCESS GAS FLOW \n   910→POWER REQUIRED \n   911→UNDER PRODUCTION LOAD (KW) \n   912→CONVEYOR TYPE \n   913→CONVEYOR STRAIGHTNESS \n   914→(IF EDGE) \n   915→CONVEYOR DIRECTION/ \n   916→RAIL JUSTIFICATION \n   917→GENERAL MACHINE QUALITY \n   918→RATING (1-10) \n   919→MANUALS AND DOCUMENTATION \n   920→RATING (1-10) \n   921→SERVICING LOCATION \n   922→COMPUTER INTERFACE AVAILABLE \n   923→ ANNUAL COST TO OPERATE \n   924→14 Section I1 - Equipment Selection \n   925→\n   926→================================================================================\n   927→PAGE 17\n   928→================================================================================\n   929→! SECTION III - RESEARCH, INC. EQUIPMENT TECHNOLOGY~ \n   930→CONVECTION OVEN THERMAL CONTROL \n   931→Convection oven thermal control requires tight control \n   932→of two variables; temperature and flow. In order to \n   933→maintain constant heat flux to the product, it is critical \n   934→that both these variables are controlled. Research, Inc.’s \n   935→MICRI-FLO XG forced convection reflow system will \n   936→be used as an example. The temperature and flow control \n   937→are defined specific to this system. \n   938→CONVECTION OVEN DESCRIPTION \n   939→Temperature control of the system must consider both \n   940→the gas and the surrounding wall temperature. Remem- \n   941→ber, even in a convection dominant heating system, the \n   942→walls radiate IR energy worth about 20%-30% of the \n   943→total heat flux of the system. The figure below shows \n   944→the heater cavity flow diagram for the MICRI-FLO XG. \n   945→The reflow and dryout zones each receive input gas \n   946→(air or nitrogen) through a series of flow meters. This \n   947→controlled flow enters a diffusion plenum behind the \n   948→heating plenum to distribute the flow. The flow then \n   949→enters a second plenum, where the gas is preheated by a \n   950→series of heaters. The heaters consist of a wound nickel- \n   951→chromium wire encased in a stainless steel jacket. The \n   952→GAS FLOW INPUT FROM \n   953→AIR OR NITROGEN SOURCE \n   954→II 4 Y \n   955→GAS FLOW INPUT FROM AIR OR NITROGEN SOURCE heaters are bent in a “U” shape such that easy removal \n   956→can occur from one side of the machine. The gas then \n   957→enters the process chamber through an array of holes. \n   958→The product is heated from a combination of the hot gas \n   959→and the hot chamber surroundings. A control thermo- \n   960→couple is mounted in each zone to maintain cavity \n   961→repeatability. A PID (Proportional - Integral - Deriva- \n   962→tive) control loop is used to maintain the cavity at \n   963→constant conditions. \n   964→The flow is removed from the reflow area by two exhaust \n   965→ducts located on each side of the reflow zone. This flow \n   966→is pulled from the zones using a high temperature, low \n   967→flow recirculating blower. The flow is pushed by the \n   968→blower through the preheater plenum arrangement. The \n   969→temperature control is accomplished in the same way as \n   970→the dryout and reflow zone. \n   971→The flow that heats the product (in all zones) is forced \n   972→through the walls in an array of holes to break up the \n   973→boundary layers on the product. The perpendicular flow \n   974→arrangement shown on page 8 is used to optimize heat \n   975→transfer. \n   976→Convection oven thermal control requires tight \n   977→control of two variables; temperature andflow. \n   978→In order to maintain constant heat flux to the \n   979→product, it is critical that both these variables \n   980→are controlled. \n   981→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology 15 \n   982→\n   983→================================================================================\n   984→PAGE 18\n   985→================================================================================\n   986→CONVECTION OVEN WITH \n   987→Convection ovens are a greater challenge to inert for \n   988→several reasons. First, the amount of flow that would be \n   989→required to transfer 80% of the energy by convection is \n   990→extremely high, and cost prohibitive. In order to mini- \n   991→mize this, recirculation ducts are added, which result in \n   992→the degradation of the purity of the source flow entering \n   993→a given zone. Higher velocities and turbulent flow are \n   994→also required, which can result in a greater opportunity \n   995→for oxygen to enter the system. Third, the pressure in the \n   996→heater cavity is positive in some areas, and negative in \n   997→others. Balancing this flow becomes adifficult task. The \n   998→job of inerting a convection oven is not impossible. \n   999→However, it should be noted that purity levels and flow \n  1000→rates are less optimum than with an IR based machine. \n  1001→CHAMBER DESCRIPTION \n  1002→Many of the same principles apply for inerting when \n  1003→comparing a convection oven to an IR oven. The MZCRZ- \n  1004→FLO XG convection oven shown below utilizes a very \n  1005→similar scheme to the 4470XN IR oven. The gas is \n  1006→brought into each zone very uniformly using the propri- VITROGEN ATMOSPHERE \n  1007→etary gas plenum distribution system. The pressure in the \n  1008→entry and exit purge sections are maintained at sufficient \n  1009→levels to minimize oxygen infiltration. Flow meter \n  1010→controlled venturi exhausts are used to balance the flow \n  1011→in the system and to remove process volatiles. \n  1012→In order to maintain the best purity levels in the zones \n  1013→where the product temperature is greater than 150°C, \n  1014→pure nitrogen is delivered to the reflow and dryout \n  1015→zone. This is a key point, as this ensures that the product \n  1016→experiences a nearly pure environment at its most criti- \n  1017→cal stages (dryout and reflow). The recirculated gas \n  1018→from these zones is reheated and used in the preheat zone. \n  1019→Here, purity is not as critical, so the use of recirculated \n  1020→gas is not detrimental to the process. \n  1021→Purity levels in the dryout and reflow zone can be \n  1022→maintained at 35 PPM over incoming purity levels at \n  1023→flow rates of 1800 cubic feet per hour (5 1 cubic meters \n  1024→per hour). \n  1025→GAS FLOW \n  1026→INPUT FROM \n  1027→N2 OR AIR \n  1028→SOURCE \n  1029→GAS FLOW \n  1030→INPUT FROM \n  1031→N2 OR AIR \n  1032→SOURCE \n  1033→16 Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology \n  1034→\n  1035→================================================================================\n  1036→PAGE 19\n  1037→================================================================================\n  1038→IR/CONVECTION OVEN CLOSED LOOP CONTROL \n  1039→One of the key oven performance issues is the ability to \n  1040→repeat a product temperature profile. Repeatability is \n  1041→influenced most by product loading. Heavy or non- \n  1042→variable loading can result in the oven incorrectly com- \n  1043→pensating, resulting in a loss of repeatability. High levels \n  1044→of repeatability can be achieved only if all the compo- \n  1045→nents of heat transfer are maintained constant. \n  1046→LAMP DEFINITION \n  1047→The closed loop heat flux control method used in a \n  1048→Research, Inc. lamp based oven will be used as an \n  1049→example of one type of control. The primary heat source \n  1050→in this system is the tungsten filament (T3) lamp. The T3 \n  1051→lamp consists of a tungsten filament encased in a sealed \n  1052→quartz envelope. The envelope is filled with an inert gas \n  1053→to keep the filament from oxidizing and failing. The \n  1054→lamps are rated for 5000 hours when operated at 100% \n  1055→capacity. In SMT applications, the lamp power never \n  1056→exceeds 20% capacity, resulting in lamp life in excess of \n  1057→20,000 hours. At typical operating setpoints of 5-15%, \n  1058→the heat transfer to the product is controlled such that \n  1059→heating rates are under 2”C/second, and the IR emission \n  1060→is in the medium to long wavelength range. \n  1061→The greatest advantage that T3 lamps have is their rapid \n  1062→response capability. Within 1 to 2 seconds after a voltage \n  1063→change occurs, the lamp is at its new temperature equi- \n  1064→librium. This rapid response is ideal for closed loop \n  1065→control, as energy can be replaced in the heating chamber \n  1066→instantly after its removal. \n  1067→CONTROL DEFINITION \n  1068→In order for the product profile to be repeatable, the total \n  1069→heat flux to the product must be held constant. The total \n  1070→heat flux to the product is the sum of IR from the lamp and \n  1071→wall plus the convective component. Refer to the figure. \n  1072→Within 1 to 2 seconds after a voltage change \n  1073→occurs, the lamp is at its new temperature \n  1074→equilibrium. This rapid response is ideal for \n  1075→closed loop control, as energy can be replaced \n  1076→in the heating chamber instantly after its \n  1077→removal. RAPID-RESPONSE (EXPOSED-TIP) \n  1078→THERMOCOUPLE \n  1079→PRODUCT ”‘7 \\ REFLF JON. $ RE-EMITTED IR \n  1080→VECTION 1 FLOW -- \\P \n  1081→PRODUCT \n  1082→Accurate contol is not achieved by maintaining only one \n  1083→of the components constant, but rather the sum of all the \n  1084→thermal energies involved. The lamp IR component is \n  1085→regulated by the SCRpower controlled system. The wall \n  1086→and convective components are collectively monitored \n  1087→by the bulk chamber temperature measured with a ther- \n  1088→mocouple probe. There are two reference setpoints; \n  1089→percent voltage for the lamp, and the bulk chamber \n  1090→temperature. The bulk chamber setpoint drives the \n  1091→equation. If the chamber is at the correct temperature, \n  1092→then the lamp setpoints will be at their setpoints. If the \n  1093→chamber is too hot, then the lamps will decrease in \n  1094→intensity. If the chamber is too cool, the lamps will \n  1095→operate at a level greater than their setpoint. The lamp \n  1096→power is modified only enough to compensate for the \n  1097→bulk chamber heat transfer deviation. In doing so, the \n  1098→total heat flux to the product is maintained. The correct \n  1099→compensation will occur providing the bulk chamber \n  1100→temperature is within k 25°C. Product temperature \n  1101→repeatability off 2.5”C will occur if the bulk chamber \n  1102→is within this defined band. \n  1103→The ability to operate within this wide band has two \n  1104→significant advantages. The first is that the oven may \n  1105→be loaded down to levels up to 80% load. The second is \n  1106→that profile changeover times are reduced significantly. \n  1107→Loading may begin when all oven temperatures are \n  1108→within f 25°C of setpoint. This requires far less time to \n  1109→achieve the “ready” state than if the “ready” process \n  1110→window was f 3”C, which is often the case inreflow oven \n  1111→operation. Warm-up times of less than 15 minutes occur. \n  1112→Profile changeover times are 3 to 5 minutes for profile \n  1113→increases of under 40”C, and under 10 minutes for profile \n  1114→decreases of under 40°C. Even in the worst case of going \n  1115→from a reflow profile to an adhesive cure profile, the \n  1116→changeover time is under 15 minutes. \n  1117→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology 17 \n  1118→\n  1119→================================================================================\n  1120→PAGE 20\n  1121→================================================================================\n  1122→IR/CONVECTION OVEN WITH NITROGEN ATMOSPHERE \n  1123→PREHEAT TOP (IR1) Infrared ovens do not require high quantities of flow to \n  1124→accomplish reflow. The predominant heat transfer oc- \n  1125→curs with IR, which does not require any flow to transfer \n  1126→the heat. The flow in the oven is used to provide some of \n  1127→the benefits of convection, which includes improving \n  1128→populated board uniformity and volatile removal. The \n  1129→amount of flow in the oven is more related to the quantity \n  1130→required to accomplish a high purity nitrogen environ- \n  1131→ment, which is considerably less than in the case of a \n  1132→convection oven. \n  1133→REFLOW \n  1134→TOP (IR5) DRY TOP (IR3) PREFLOW TOP (IR4) NITROGEN OVEN DESCRIPTION \n  1135→The 4470XN from Research, Inc. is an example of an IR \n  1136→dominant oven that provides a high purity nitrogen \n  1137→environment at low flow levels. The system uses a \n  1138→proprietary gas flow distribution method through the \n  1139→chamber walls to uniformly blanket the chamber with the \n  1140→process nitrogen. A slight positive pressure inside the \n  1141→chamber helps minimize external room effects and helps \n  1142→keep oxygen out of the chamber. Entry and exit purge \n  1143→sections are present to provide a nitrogen curtain at the \n  1144→chamber entrance and exit, which also assists in keeping \n  1145→oxygen out of the chamber. Flow meter controlled \n  1146→venturi exhausts are used to balance the flow in the \n  1147→system and to remove process volatiles. \n  1148→PREHEAT BOTTOM (IR2) The gas system is non-recirculatory, in that the gas is \n  1149→used once and then expended. This has the advantage \n  1150→that process contaminants are not recirculated which \n  1151→helps maintain high nitrogen purity levels. The heat \n  1152→chamber is accessible by unfastening six clamps and \n  1153→elevating the top heater vertically through the use of a \n  1154→crank mechanism. Purity levels of 20 PPM (over in- \n  1155→coming purity level) are attainable in all heating zones. \n  1156→Flow rates are in the 1200 to 1600 cubic feet per hour \n  1157→(34 to 45 cubic meters per hour) range. Machines in the \n  1158→field are experiencing total purity levels of 10-15 PPM \n  1159→(including incoming) at flow rates of 1300 cubic feet per \n  1160→hour (37 cubic meters per hour). These purity levels are \n  1161→measured with the machine fully loaded with product. \n  1162→REFLOW \n  1163→BOTTOM (1R6) DRY BOTTOM (IR3) PREFLOW BOTTOM (IR4) ~~~ ~ \n  1164→The amount of flow in the oven is more \n  1165→related to the quantity required to accom- \n  1166→plish a high purity nitrogen environment, \n  1167→which is considerably less than in the case \n  1168→of a convection oven. \n  1169→NOTE: VENTURI EXHAUSTS ARE ACTIVATED BY COMPRESSED AIR, \n  1170→ALL OTHERS MAY BE NITROGEN OR COMPRESSED AIR. \n  1171→18 Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology \n  1172→\n  1173→================================================================================\n  1174→PAGE 21\n  1175→================================================================================\n  1176→EDGE CONVEYOR \n  1177→Proper design of the edge conveying system is critical, \n  1178→as typically only 0.17 inches (4 mm) is in contact with \n  1179→the bottom side edges of the product. If the conveyor \n  1180→has lateral warpage of greater than this amount, the \n  1181→product will fall into the oven. Thus, edge conveyor \n  1182→straightness is a key issue in SMT processing. \n  1183→As the conveyor rails heat during the machine warm-up \n  1184→period, they will grow and tend to warp. The patented \n  1185→system from Research, Inc. shown below tensions the \n  1186→rails, which pulls the rail assembly straight. The lever \n  1187→(outlined in dark) is attached to the rail. The bottom side \n  1188→of the lever is attached to an adjustable spring mechanism \n  1189→that runs from end to end in the machine. Approximately \n  1190→1500 lbs (681 kg) of tensioning force is applied to each rail to maintain straightness. The machine frame is made \n  1191→of welded structural tubing in order to maintain its \n  1192→structural integrity. \n  1193→The use of the tensioning system allows the mass of the \n  1194→rails to be maintained at a reasonably low value, which \n  1195→minimizes the thermal affect of the rail on the product. \n  1196→As the conveyo’r rails heat during the ma- \n  1197→chine warm-up period, they will grow and \n  1198→tend to warp. The patented system from \n  1199→Research, Inc. shown below tensions the \n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"fbdb10ba-9e50-4e47-8a94-a4cee9d5e4dc","timestamp":"2026-02-07T06:10:16.350Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"152169ae-16da-4fe3-8133-0afad6f5587e"}
