{"parentUuid":"ef3cf381-f473-49ed-9150-4ce3d2d3361d","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"c1ed167a-e051-4050-bb99-05ea85646cca","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a4d6301","slug":"gentle-weaving-manatee","type":"progress","data":{"type":"hook_progress","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","hookName":"PostToolUse:Read","command":"callback"},"parentToolUseID":"toolu_vrtx_01Xow8tz9SVKy3FjiRRJnxcV","toolUseID":"toolu_vrtx_01Xow8tz9SVKy3FjiRRJnxcV","timestamp":"2026-02-07T06:10:21.943Z","uuid":"c664ba65-21e8-4b2d-8b30-0357c38fbdb9"}
{"parentUuid":"c664ba65-21e8-4b2d-8b30-0357c38fbdb9","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"c1ed167a-e051-4050-bb99-05ea85646cca","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a4d6301","slug":"gentle-weaving-manatee","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01Hs5yg1Gd91DhgriwzTy2xd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have the complete content of the handbook. Let me provide a comprehensive summary of all the extracted content organized by the topics you requested.\n\n---\n\n## COMPLETE CONTENT EXTRACTION: SMT Reflow Soldering Handbook\n\n**Author:** Norman R. Cox, M.S.M.E.  \n**Publisher:** Research, Inc., 1992  \n**File:** `/app/data/handbook.pdf` (24 pages)\n\n---\n\n### 1. PREHEAT DEFINITION, TEMPERATURE REGION, RAMP RATE, AND ALLOWABLE LIMITS\n\n**Preheat Definition (Page 5):**\nA typical profile consists of a **preheat, dryout, and reflow** section (zone) where different heating rates are used. In the preheat section, the goal is to fully preheat the SMT assembly.\n\n**Temperature Region:**\n- Preheat temperatures are generally between **100 degrees C to 150 degrees C**.\n\n**Ramp Rate Calculation and Limits:**\n- The most critical parameter in the preheat section is to minimize the rate of rise to **less than 2 degrees C/second**.\n- The main concern is minimizing thermal shock on the components of the assembly.\n- Multilayer ceramic chip capacitors can be vulnerable to **cracking if heated too fast**.\n- Solder paste can **spatter** also if heated too fast.\n- Chip capacitors may crack if the rise rate in the preheat zone is excessive.\n\n**Heat Transfer Equation (Page 8):**\nThe temperature rise of an object subjected to heat is determined by:\n\n```\nAT = Q x A x t / (M x Cp)\n\nWHERE:\nAT = Product Temperature Rise (degrees C)\nQ  = Heat Absorbed (w/cm2)\nA  = Exposed Area (cm2)\nt  = Heating Dwell Time (seconds)\nM  = Mass of Object (kg)\nCp = Specific Heat (w-sec/kg-degrees C)\n```\n\nThe mass (M) times the specific heat (Cp) is referred to as **thermal mass**. Greater thermal mass requires more heat to achieve a given temperature rise.\n\n---\n\n### 2. SOLDER LIQUIDUS TEMPERATURE, TAL MEASUREMENT, TAL WINDOW (MIN/MAX)\n\n**Solder Liquidus (Melting) Temperature (Page 5):**\n- For **63/37 Eutectic solder**, the melting temperature is **183 degrees C**.\n- This temperature must be exceeded by approximately **20 degrees C** to ensure quality reflow (i.e., the solder joint must reach approximately **203 degrees C or higher**).\n\n**TAL (Time Above Liquidus) -- referred to as \"Wetting Time\" in this handbook (Page 5):**\n- The amount of time the solder joint is above the melting point is referred to as the **\"wetting time\"**.\n- **TAL Window:** The wetting time is normally **30 to 60 seconds** for most pastes.\n- **Minimum:** 30 seconds\n- **Maximum:** 60 seconds\n- If the wetting time is **excessive**, intermetallics may occur in the joint, which result in **brittle solder joints**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. PEAK TEMPERATURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n**Peak Temperature (Page 5):**\n- Solder reflow is accomplished in the reflow zone, where the solder paste is elevated to a temperature **greater than the melting point** of the solder.\n- For 63/37 Eutectic solder (melting point 183 degrees C), the temperature must be exceeded by approximately **20 degrees C**, implying a peak temperature target of approximately **203 degrees C**.\n- In the typical profile diagram, the temperature axis goes up to approximately **280 degrees C** on the scale, but the actual reflow peak is in the ~200-220 degrees C range.\n\n**Excessive Peak Temperature Effects:**\n- Excessive heating in the reflow zone may result in **burnt boards**, **damaged components**, or possibly **solder dewetting**.\n- In a dewet solder joint, the solder is reflowed but it is pulled away from the pad or the component lead.\n\n**Insufficient Peak Temperature Effects:**\n- Insufficient heat will result in **non-reflowed or partially reflowed solder joints**. The joints will have a **dull, rough surface finish**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. BOARD GEOMETRY, THERMAL DWELL REQUIREMENTS, CONVEYOR SPEED REQUIREMENTS\n\n**Board Geometry and Mass Differential (Pages 6, 8):**\n- The three main causes of non-uniform heating in the SMT reflow process include:\n  1. **Mass differential on the product**\n  2. **Conveyor and heater edge effects**\n  3. **Product loading**\n- Areas populated with large components (e.g., PLCCs) have considerably more thermal mass per unit area than areas with discrete components, making them more difficult to heat.\n- If two very large components are in close proximity to each other, the **geometric view factor** to a solder joint between them is decreased, making it more difficult to heat by IR.\n\n**Profile Process Band (Page 7):**\n- The profile is not a line, but rather a **band or process window**.\n- The band size is defined by the range of temperature deviation that can occur during reflow and yield high quality results.\n- Example band size: **25 degrees C**.\n- The actual size of the band will vary depending upon **solder paste**, **component type**, and **circuit board material**.\n- The sum of all non-uniformities (component thermal mismatch, oven loading, and oven uniformity) must fall within the defined reflow profile band.\n\n**Thermal Dwell / Process Dwell Time (Page 14):**\n- The process speed equation is:\n\n```\nPROCESS SPEED = OVEN CHAMBER HEATED LENGTH / PROCESS DWELL TIME\n```\n\n**Conveyor Speed Requirements (Page 14):**\n- The minimum line speed is calculated as:\n\n```\nLINE SPEED (MINIMUM) = BOARDS PER MINUTE x LENGTH PER BOARD / LOAD FACTOR\n```\n\n- Operating at a conveyor speed greater than the calculated speed ensures that no product jams will occur on the onload area of the reflow oven.\n- The process speed must be greater than the minimum calculated line speed to ensure that bottlenecks will not occur at the reflow oven.\n\n**Loading Factor (Page 8):**\n- Loading Factor = L / (L + S), where L = product length in oven, S = spacing between products.\n- The higher the value of the loading factor, the more difficult it is for any oven to give repeatable results.\n- Ovens will generally specify a maximum loading factor ranging from **0.5 to 0.9**.\n\n**Product Matrix Process Speeds (Page 22):**\nVarious oven models show standard process speeds ranging from:\n- **9-12 in/min** to **36-50 in/min** (standard length)\n- **20-30 cm/min** to **90-130 cm/min** (standard length)\n\n---\n\n### 5. THERMOCOUPLE SELECTION GUIDANCE\n\n**Which TC to Use as Representative (Pages 8, 17, 19):**\n\n**For Mass Differential Measurement (Page 8):**\n- The non-uniformity caused by thermal mass differential may be measured by **thermocoupling a lead on the largest and smallest device**, and running a profile.\n\n**For Oven Uniformity Measurement (Page 8):**\n- The oven uniformity may be measured by **thermocoupling a bare board over a matrix of points**, and running a profile.\n- The matrix should include points on the **board edges (front, back, left, and right)**, and the **center of the board**.\n\n**For Oven Repeatability Measurement (Page 8):**\n- Run a profile on a test board, then load the oven down. Periodically, run the test board within the oven load and compare with the unloaded profile.\n\n**Control Thermocouple (Page 17):**\n- A control thermocouple is mounted in each zone to maintain cavity repeatability.\n- A PID (Proportional - Integral - Derivative) control loop is used to maintain the cavity at constant conditions.\n\n**Rapid-Response Thermocouple (Page 19):**\n- A rapid-response (exposed-tip) thermocouple is used in the IR/Convection oven closed loop control system to monitor bulk chamber temperature.\n- There are two reference setpoints: percent voltage for the lamp, and the bulk chamber temperature.\n- Product temperature repeatability of **+/- 2.5 degrees C** will occur if the bulk chamber is within **+/- 25 degrees C** of setpoint.\n\n---\n\n### 6. OTHER PROCESS PARAMETERS, LIMITS, AND SPECIFICATIONS\n\n**Dryout/Soak Zone (Page 5):**\n- The second heating section (referred to as the dryout, soak, or preflow zone) is used primarily to ensure that the solder paste is fully dried before hitting reflow temperatures.\n- This zone also acts as a **flux activation zone** for some solder pastes.\n- Three \"typical\" profile bands exist for **RMA**, **water soluble**, and **no-clean** solder pastes.\n\n**Heating Zones Required (Page 6):**\n- Reflowing solder paste typically requires **two or three different heating rates** to accomplish the process.\n- All pastes could be successfully reflowed using **three zones**.\n- Independent top/bottom heating control helps with dual conveyors at different heights, and with product warpage minimization, but most often top and bottom heaters will be set at the same value.\n- Edge heating zones (one front, one back) compensate for edge conveyors acting as heat sinks.\n\n**Heating Modes (Page 9):**\n- Three heating modes: **convection**, **conduction**, and **infrared radiation (IR)**.\n- IR dominant systems: convection/IR ratio of **60/40 to 40/60**.\n- Convection dominant systems: ratios of **70/30 to 80/20**.\n\n**Infrared Heating Equation (Page 10):**\n```\nQ = V x Es x At x K x (Ts^4 - Tt^4)\n\nWHERE:\nQ  = Infrared heat transfer (w/cm2)\nV  = Geometric view factor (0-1)\nEs = Emissivity factor of the source (0-1)\nAt = Absorptivity factor of the target (0-1)\nK  = Stefan-Boltzmann Constant (5.67 x 10^-12 w/cm2/K^4)\nTs = Source temperature (K)\nTt = Target temperature (K)\n```\n- View factors in SMT reflow: **0.90 to 0.95**.\n- Source emissivity and target absorptivity: **0.90 to 0.95** for most SMT applications.\n- Solder paste is an excellent absorber of infrared energy.\n\n**Convection Heating Equation (Page 11):**\n```\nQ = H x AT\n\nWHERE:\nQ  = Heat absorbed per unit area (w/cm2)\nH  = Convective film coefficient (W/cm2-degrees C)\nAT = Temperature difference between fluid and object (degrees C)\n```\n\n**Nitrogen Atmosphere Requirements (Page 13):**\n- Significant reduction in board discoloration: **10,000 ppm** oxygen\n- Reduction in solder balling (RMA): **200 ppm** oxygen\n- Reflow of low-residue solder paste: **< 100 ppm** oxygen\n- Fine pitch soldering and no-clean fluxes are the two major process changes driving the need for nitrogen atmospheres.\n\n**Nitrogen Purity Levels Achievable (Pages 18, 20):**\n- Convection oven (MICRI-FLO XG): **35 PPM over incoming purity** at flow rates of 1800 cubic feet per hour (51 cubic meters per hour).\n- IR/Convection oven (4470XN): **20 PPM over incoming purity** in all heating zones, at flow rates of 1200-1600 cubic feet per hour (34-45 cubic meters per hour). Machines in the field: **10-15 PPM total** (including incoming) at 1300 CFH (37 cubic meters per hour).\n\n**IR/Convection Oven Closed Loop Control (Page 19):**\n- T3 lamps rated for **5000 hours** at 100% capacity; in SMT applications, lamp power never exceeds **20% capacity**, resulting in lamp life in excess of **20,000 hours**.\n- At typical operating setpoints of **5-15%**, heating rates are under **2 degrees C/second** and IR emission is in the medium to long wavelength range.\n- Lamp response time: **1 to 2 seconds** to new temperature equilibrium.\n- Product temperature repeatability of **+/- 2.5 degrees C** when bulk chamber is within **+/- 25 degrees C**.\n- Maximum loading: up to **80% load factor**.\n- Warm-up times: less than **15 minutes**.\n- Profile changeover times: **3 to 5 minutes** for increases under 40 degrees C; under **10 minutes** for decreases under 40 degrees C; under **15 minutes** worst case.\n\n**Edge Conveyor (Page 21):**\n- Typically only **0.17 inches (4 mm)** of the conveyor is in contact with the bottom side edges of the product.\n- If the conveyor has lateral warpage greater than this amount, the product will fall into the oven.\n- Approximately **1500 lbs (681 kg)** of tensioning force is applied to each rail to maintain straightness.\n\n**Selection Criteria Chart (Page 16):** A blank comparison chart for Vendor A/B/C including:\n- Process band width required (degrees C)\n- Mass differential (degrees C)\n- Oven uniformity (degrees C)\n- Repeatability (degrees C)\n- Maximum loading factor\n- Process speed\n- Process gas type\n- Dryout zone purity (PPM)\n- Process gas flow\n- Power required under production load (KW)\n- Conveyor type and straightness\n- Conveyor direction/rail justification\n- General machine quality rating (1-10)\n- Manuals and documentation rating (1-10)\n- Computer interface available\n- Annual cost to operate\n\n**Cost Analysis Example (Page 15):**\n- Hot Gas (Convection): $50,000 initial, 1800 CFH gas usage, 20 KW power, $37,070 total annual cost\n- IR: $60,000 initial, 1200 CFH gas usage, 12 KW power, $31,804 total annual cost\n- Capital Recovery Factor (CRF) at 10% interest over 5 years: **0.2638**\n- Assumed operating hours per year: **2080 hours**\n\n**References:**\n1. \"The Transformation of American Industry\", Productivity-Quality Systems, Inc. Module #1, 1986.\n2. \"Radiation Plus Convection: The Total Solution\", Norman R. Cox, Research Inc., NEPCON West 1990.\n3. \"Optimization of Solder Reflow with IR\", Norman R. Cox, Research, Inc., ISHM 1985.\n4. \"Inert IR Reflow: The Significance of Oxygen Concentration in the Atmosphere\", Colin Lea, National Physics Laboratory, Surface Mount International, 1991.\n5. Managerial and Engineering Economy, George A. Taylor, D. Van Nostrand Company, 1975.\n\n---\n\nThis is the complete text content of the 24-page handbook. Note that some pages contained only images, diagrams, or product branding (Pages 1, 2, 23, 24) with minimal or no extractable text. The handbook is authored by Norman R. Cox of Research, Inc. (Minneapolis, MN) and dates to 1992. It is fundamentally a guide to SMT reflow soldering process engineering, covering thermal profiling fundamentals, equipment selection, and Research Inc.'s specific oven technologies."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1954,"cache_read_input_tokens":53294,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1954,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXtDE9BgkcEMUNpgjXL54","type":"assistant","uuid":"3723141d-d0d9-4a55-9ee9-7307e9009911","timestamp":"2026-02-07T06:11:54.077Z"}
